            </div>
            <div class="project-card-expanded">
              <div class="project-card-details">
                <div class="project-detail-block">
                  <span class="project-detail-label">Overview</span>
                  <p>Automated Linux system monitoring with cron-scheduled Bash scripts using AWK and grep to collect CPU, memory, disk, and network metrics every 60 seconds, trigger threshold-based alerts, and compress logs automatically.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="project-detail-block">
                  <span class="project-detail-label">Key Features</span>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Cron-scheduled collection of CPU, memory, disk I/O, and network metrics at 60-second intervals</li>
                    <li>AWK/grep-based parsing of /proc and system utilities for lightweight metric extraction</li>
                    <li>Configurable threshold-based alerting for resource utilization spikes</li>
                    <li>Automated log rotation, compression (gzip), and cleanup with retention policies</li>
                    <li>Zero-dependency Bash implementation — runs on any standard Linux system</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="project-detail-block">
                  <span class="project-detail-label">Architecture</span>
                  <p>Cron daemon triggers Bash scripts every minute. AWK processes /proc/stat, /proc/meminfo, df, and /proc/net/dev for metric extraction. Grep filters for alert conditions. Structured JSON/CSV logging with automated gzip compression and age-based cleanup. Designed for production reliability with minimal overhead.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="project-detail-block">
                  <span class="project-detail-label">Impact</span>
                  <p class="project-card-metric">60-second metric collection · 4 core resource types (CPU, memory, disk, network) · Threshold-based alerting · Automated log compression & cleanup · Zero external dependencies</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
